al state  but we could describe it as a tolerance  for ambiguity  when you started graduate school several  years ago you  may remember my telling you that the  romantic  poet john keats called this state  negative capability or the capacity to  thrive  in the midst of uncertainty and doubt  keats and others after him understood  negative capability as a prerequisite  for discovery and creativity and  although it may be discomforting at  times  you have probably found that to be true  for yourself as well  to become the scholars and researchers  you are and aspire to be  you know you know you need to be  comfortable  even excited when faced with uncertainty  and mystery to push forward the  boundaries of what we know  and and know and understand about the  world  we have to be a bit unsettled by it  there are other powerful competencies  you you have  skills that are attractive to potential  employers  in addition to making you better a  better researcher and  an intrepid seeker of knowledge you  know how to tap into your innate  curiosity  and to confront complicated problems in  a systematic way  you can manage the twists and turns of  research  work with others who may hold different  perspectives from your own  and and communicate your ideas with  clarity  and grace you have the ability to  identify a problem or an issue and  pursue it  until a solution is found or knowledge  is gained  without being discouraged well mostly  without being discouraged  and your skills in problem solving  embracing differences  and building constructive friendships  will help you when faced with the  vicissitudes of work  and personal life anyone can have a  passion for an idea or a subject  but a graduate student learns how to  carry it forward with determination and  optimism  this is the very reason a graduate  degree matters  during a pandemic or any other time our  unwavering commitment to critical  inquiry  intellectual rigor and creative problem  solving  give us what we need to confront any  difficulty  address any unexpected development  and the world needs you right now  to apply what you have learned to the  most intractable questions and  predicaments of our time  i invited professor iwasaki to talk with  you today  not only because she's an inspiring  speaker  but because she's an example of someone  who uses her training to actively  solve a problem of great complexity even  as it unfolds  it is true that we are living through an  extraordinary time  but i wish to leave you with the good  news  that you are ideally prepared to face  today's challenges  and any that may arise in the future  you can work your way towards solutions  using the skills you have learned here  at yale  we confer your degrees upon you in all  confidence  looking forward to see what you will  accomplish  you leave yale as thinkers explorers  creators and teachers inventive  and original scholars leaders and  communicators  whatever direction you choose let your  accomplishments  inspire you to tackle new challenges  knowing  you have what it takes to confront the  unforeseen  it has been my privilege to lead the  graduate school's  efforts to provide you with the  environment that helped you grow into  the scholars  you are today i'm hugely proud of what  you have accomplished and excited to see  what you will do next  congratulations  and now to close our ceremony i'd like  to invite chaplain  sharon coogler for her parting words to  you and to benediction  at the end of her wonderful book  wintering  catherine may writes about the annual  gaelic festival  of imblok which takes place  in early spring this celebration  involves dusting away the cobwebs and  other remnants  of the long winter months and examines  what it takes to emerge  from a relentlessly tough season  a season reminiscent of graduate studies  with a global pandemic  superimposed over it intellectual  emotional and spiritual labor has been  the constant  with uncertainty dwelling in dark  corners  may writes about the unfurling of new  leaves  and learning to cope with the debris of  a long  disordered season she gives us this to  ponder  but we are brave and a new world awaits  us  gleaming and green  alive with the beat of wings  and besides we have a kind of gospel to  tell now  and a duty to share it we who have  wintered  have learned some things we sing it out  like birds  we let our voices fill the air  my beloved yale family you have learned  some things you have done it  and at times it has felt impossible but  you have crawled through  a long hard disordered season  now go forth with joy for you have made  it  you have a duty to share what you have  learned and to sing it out like birds  we take leave of this place filled with  gratitude for our families  friends teachers and guides  we are mindful of those we cherish who  are not here  and whose presence is sorely missed  today though  we are here we are alive  with the beat of wings on this day  and every day forward may all that is  holy  shine upon us and tenderly bless  our every breath may we go  in shalom in salam  in shanti may we go in peace  amen  [Music]  [Music]  [Music]  [Music]  my  [Music]  [Music]  [Music]  [Music]  [Music]  um  you


